PR', 'Avai-SC', 'Bahia-BA', 'Botafogo-RJ', 'Ceara-CE']\n\nCup rows: 1337 seasons: 2012 2021\ncup rounds: ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8']\n\nLib rows: 1255 stages: ['final', 'group stage', 'quarterfinals', 'round of 16', 'semifinals']\nlib goal vals sample: [('1', 380), ('2', 311), ('0', 297), ('3', 144), ('4', 77)]\nlib rows with empty goals: 0\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef rows(p):\n    with open(p, newline='', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nbr = rows('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nprint(\"Brasileirao rows:\", len(br), \"seasons:\", sorted({r['season'] for r in br})[:3], \"...\", sorted({r['season'] for r in br})[-3:])\nprint(\"home sample:\", sorted({r['home_team'] for r in br})[:8])\n\ncup = rows('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\nprint(\"\\nCup rows:\", len(cup), \"seasons:\", min(r['season'] for r in cup), max(r['season'] for r in cup))\nprint(\"cup rounds:\", sorted({r['round'] for r in cup}, key=lambda x: (len(x), x))[:12])\n\nlib = rows('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nprint(\"\\nLib rows:\", len(lib), \"stages:\", sorted({r['stage'] for r in lib}))\nprint(\"lib goal vals sample:\", Counter(r['home_goal'] for r in lib).most_common(5))\nempty_goals = sum(1 for r in lib if not r['home_goal'].strip() or not r['away_goal'].strip())\nprint(\"lib rows with empty goals:\", empty_goals)\nEOF","time":{"start":1788230483911,"end":1788230483917}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"mcp 1.29.1 with FastMCP works. Now let me plan the implementation.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```\n├── TASK.md (existing)\n├── README.md (update at end)\n├── requirements.txt\n├── pyproject.toml (optional)\n├── server.py                  # MCP server entry point (stdio)\n├── brazilian_soccer/          # main package\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── data_loader.py         # loads all 6 CSVs into normalized models\n│   ├── models.py              # Match, Player dataclasses\n│   ├── normalize.py           # team name normalization, date parsing, slug\n│   ├── store.py               # in-memory knowledge store + indexes\n│   ├── analytics.py           # stats: standings, h2h, team stats, aggregates\n│   └── tools.py               # MCP tool implementations (returns JSON-able dicts / text)\n├── tests/\n│   ├── conftest.py\n│   ├── test_bdd_match_queries.py    # GWT/BDD-style pytest\n│   ├── test_bdd_team_queries.py\n│   ├── test_bdd_player_queries.py\n│   ├── test_bdd_competition_queries.py\n│   ├── test_bdd_statistics.py\n│   ├── test_normalize.py\n│   └── test_server.py               # MCP server end-to-end via in-memory client\n```\n\nSince pandas isn't installed, I'll use the stdlib `csv` module. That keeps dependencies minimal (only `mcp` + pytest for tests). Good for the \"lightweight\" requirement and fast loading.\n\n### Data notes:\n\n1. **Brasileirao_Matches.csv**: datetime ISO w/ time, home_team with state suffix \"Palmeiras-SP\", home_team_state, away_team..., home_goal, away_goal, season, round. Competition: \"Brasileirão Serie A\".\n\n2. **Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv**: round (number as string in quotes? first col quoted), datetime, home_team, away_team, home_goal, away_goal, season. Note home_team has formats like \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\", \"América - MG\". Competition: \"Copa do Brasil\". Round numbers — the finals would be the last round (5 or 6). Actually cup rounds: the spec says \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" — round column is numeric; final = max round within season. I can support filtering round=\"final\" by resolving to max round per season.\n\n3. **Libertadores_Matches.csv**: datetime, home_team, away_team, home_goal (strings!), away_goal (strings), season, stage. Competition \"Copa Libertadores\". Note goals may be empty or \"2\" strings. Also possibly penalty info? Let me check for special values.\n\n4. **BR-Football-Dataset.csv**: tournament, home, home_goal (float), away_goal, away, corners, attacks, shots, time, date (ISO date), ht_diff, at_diff, ht_result, at_result, total_corners. Tournaments include \"Copa do Brasil\", \"Brasileirão Serie A\"? Let me check unique tournaments. This file has extra stats. It overlaps other files but we'll treat it as its own dataset (\"BR Football\" extended stats).\n\n5. **novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv**: ID, Data (DD/MM/YYYY), Ano, Rodada, Equipe_mandante, Equipe_visitante, Gols_mandante, Gols_visitante, Mandante_UF, Visitante_UF, Vencedor, Arena, OBS. Competition \"Brasileirão Serie B\"? Actually it's \"Campeonato Brasileiro\" 2003-2019 — it includes both Serie A and Serie B possibly? Let me check. The row shows \"Guarani\" vs \"Vasco\" — 2003 Serie A had 24 teams. Hmm, 6,886 matches for 2003-2019 (17 seasons). Serie A had 380-469 matches/season (with 2003-2005 having more rounds). Actually 2003-2005: 24 teams double round-robin = 552 matches/season. 2006+: 20 teams = 380/season. 552*3 + 380*14 = 1656 + 5320 = 6976. Close to 6,886. So it's Serie A. Good.\n\nWait, but there might be duplicates: Brasileirao_Matches.csv covers 2012-2023ish (4,180 matches). Let me check its seasons. And novo_campeonato 2003-2019. They overlap 2012-2019. That's fine — they're separate datasets; queries can filter by competition. I'll keep them distinct (competition names: \"Brasileirão\" for both but different sources...). Hmm.\n\nActually for dedup and the \"knowledge graph\" concept: the spec wants a knowledge graph interface. I'll build an in-memory graph: nodes = teams, players, competitions, matches; edges = played_in, part_of, etc. But primarily a query layer over the structured data with normalization. I'll implement a lightweight knowledge graph: \n- `graph.py`: Node/Edge stores linking teams → matches → competitions → seasons, and players → clubs → teams (fuzzy link between FIFA club names and match team names).\n- Cross-file queries: e.g., \"top-rated players at Flamengo\" links FIFA club to match team via normalization.\n\nLet me check data specifics:\n- Brasileirao_Matches seasons range\n- Libertadores goal values (strings, maybe \"2 (4)\" penalties?), empty goals\n- BR-Football tournaments unique values\n- fifa Nationality for Brazil, clubs\n\nLet me inspect quickly.\n\n### MCP Tools (per Required Capabilities):\n\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=None)` — match queries.\n2. `get_head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)` — h2h.\n3. `get_team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` — W/D/L, goals.\n4. `list_teams(competition=None)` or `get_team_history`.\n5. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=None)`.\n6. `get_player(name)` — exact/best match.\n7. `get_standings(competition, season, top=None)` — standings table.\n8. `get_competitions()` — list competitions + seasons coverage.\n9. `get_statistics(scope..., metric...)` — avg goals per match, home win rate, biggest wins, best home/away records.\n10. `get_derby_matches(season=None)` — traditional rivalries (Fla-Flu, Majestoso, Choque-Rei, Clássico dos Milhões, Grenal, Atletiba, Ba-Vi, Dérbi Paulista, etc.).\n11. Maybe `answer_question(natural_language)` — a simple NL router to the above. The spec says \"MCP server connected to an LLM for query processing\" — the LLM does the query processing by choosing tools. But a bonus `query` tool that does lightweight keyword routing could help demos. I'll add a simple `natural_language_query` tool that routes to capabilities based on keywords. Keep it deterministic and tested lightly.\n\nAlso resources: maybe expose `soccer://teams`, `soccer://competitions`. And a `db_status` resource. Keep it simple.\n\n### Normalization\n\n- Strip state suffixes: \"-SP\", \" - RJ\", \" (URU)\", \"(EQU)\", etc. Actually Libertadores has country codes \"(URU)\" — those are foreign clubs; keep country tag as-is but normalize the name part.\n- Remove accent folding for matching (casefold + unicodedata NFKD strip accents).\n- Canonical team mapping: alias table for common variants: \"Sao Paulo\" → \"São Paulo\", \"Athletico-PR\" → \"Athletico Paranaense\"? Hmm — careful: I want consistent canonical names but shouldn't over-engineer. Strategy:\n  - `canonical_name(raw)`: strip state suffix (regex `\\s*[-–]?\\s*(AC|AL|AP|AM|BA|CE|DF|ES|GO|MA|MT|MS|MG|PA|PB|PR|PE|PI|RJ|RN|RS|RO|RR|SC|SP|SE|TO)$` after removing trailing whitespace and punctuation), strip parenthetical country codes, strip accents, collapse whitespace, title-case-ish canonical display.\n  - Alias dict for known variants: \"Sao Paulo\" → \"São Paulo\", \"Atletico Paranaense\"/\"Athletico-PR\"/\"Athletico Paranaense\" → \"Athletico-PR\"? \n\nLet me look at actual team names in the datasets to build a good alias table. Key: same team across files should map to same canonical key. Display name: pick the most common raw form (without state suffix) or a curated display name.\n\nI'll build:\n- `normalize.py`: \n  - `strip_accents(s)`\n  - `canonical_key(s)` → stable slug for matching\n  - `clean_team_name(s)` → display name without state suffix\n  - `parse_date(s)` → date or None (handles ISO, DD/MM/YYYY, with time)\n  - `parse_int(s)` → int or None\n\n- Alias resolution: The team registry builds canonical display names by picking the longest/most common clean name among variants. E.g., clean forms: \"Palmeiras\" (from \"Palmeiras-SP\"), \"Palmeiras\" (BR-Football). \"Corinthians\" vs \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" — full names appear in some datasets? Let me check what names exist. I'll add a curated alias map for well-known variants to guarantee consistency (e.g., \"Sao Paulo FC\" → \"São Paulo\", \"Cruzeiro EC\" → \"Cruzeiro\", \"Atlético-MG\"/\"Atletico Mineiro\"/\"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"Atlético-MG\"?). \n\nI need to check the actual names in each file. Let me sample unique team names.\n\n### Analytics\n\n- standings(competition, season): group matches by season for Brasileirão (round-robin). Points 3/1/0, tiebreak: points, wins, goal diff, goals for. Mark champion (top of table at final round). Note: some datasets' Libertadores/Copa aren't league format — standings only for league competitions (Brasileirão). I'll compute standings for \"Brasileirão\" competitions (both sources) and refuse gracefully for cups.\n- h2h(team_a, team_b): all matches between the two across competitions, with wins/draws/losses, goal totals, list of matches.\n- team_stats(team, season?, competition?, venue?): matches, W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate, home/away split.\n- aggregates: avg goals per match, home win rate, biggest wins (sorted by |goal diff|), best home record, best away record, per-season comparison.\n\n### Performance\nLoad once at server start; ~23k matches + 18k players from CSV via stdlib csv — should be < 2s. Keep indexes: matches_by_team (dict canonical → list), by_competition_season, players sorted by overall. Simple lookups fast.\n\n### MCP server\n- `server.py` with FastMCP, stdio transport. Tools defined returning JSON strings (or structured dicts — FastMCP converts to text). I'll return JSON strings for consistency, plus `text` formatted summaries? Simpler: return JSON-serializable dicts; FastMCP serializes them. Hmm, FastMCP tool returning dict → it will be converted via pydantic to text? In mcp 1.x, FastMCP tools return content blocks; dict results get JSON-serialized automatically (yes, `_convert_to_content` handles dict/list → json.dumps). I'll return dicts.\n\nAlso add resources:\n- `soccer://status` — dataset stats.\n- `soccer://teams` — canonical team list.\n- `soccer://competitions` — competitions and seasons.\n\nAnd a prompt? Maybe not needed. Keep focused.\n\n### Tests (BDD GWT pytest)\npytest-bdd isn't installed; installing it is possible (network works). But simpler and more robust: write BDD-style tests with plain pytest — scenario docstrings in Given/When/Then style with `scenario` step functions. The spec's \"Testing Approach\" says BDD scenarios in Gherkin; prompts.txt mentions \"test using BDD GWT structured PyTest\". I could install pytest-bdd and write real .feature files. That's nice fidelity to the ask. Let me install pytest-bdd.\n\nGiven network works, install pytest-bdd. Write features/*.feature files + step definitions. That satisfies \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\".\n\nTest files:\n- tests/features/match_queries.feature\n- tests/features/team_queries.feature\n- tests/features/player_queries.feature\n- tests/features/competition_queries.feature\n- tests/features/statistics.feature\n- tests/features/data_quality.feature (normalization: team name variants, date formats, UTF-8)\n- tests/step_defs/test_*.py\n\nPlus unit tests for normalization, and an MCP end-to-end test using the in-memory client (mcp ClientSession with memory transport) to verify tools/list and a couple of tool calls work. That proves \"the MCP server builds and runs\".\n\nAlso performance checks (simple lookups < 2s, aggregates < 5s) — include a perf smoke test with generous assertion.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Inspect data more (unique team names, date formats, weird values).\n2. Write normalize.py, models.py, data_loader.py, store.py, analytics.py, tools.py, server.py.\n3. Write feature files + steps + unit tests.\n4. requirements.txt + README update.\n5. Run tests, fix issues.\n\nLet me look deeper at the data first. Check Libertadores goals values, BR-Football tournaments, and team name variants.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ad7bf8f001cLSLl2Rhc9zuwv","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","messageID":"msg_05ad62bfc001wlM0hkrDp7SVWi"}}
