ting Cristal', 'São Paulo', 'Talleres', 'The Strongest', 'Tigre', 'Tigres', 'Tijuana', 'Tolima', 'Toluca', 'Trujillanos (VEN)', 'Universidad Católica', 'Universidad de Chile', 'Universidad de Concepción', 'Universitario (PER)', 'Universitario de Sucre', 'Universitario-PER', 'Unión Española', 'Unión La Calera', 'Vasco', 'Vélez Sarsfield', 'Zamora', 'Zulia']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef rows(p):\n    with open(p, newline='', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nlib = rows('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nnames = set()\nfor r in lib:\n    names.add(r['home_team']); names.add(r['away_team'])\nprint(len(names), \"libertadores names\")\nprint(sorted(names))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788230597451,"end":1788230597454}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"625 unique names. This confirms the pattern: base name + state suffix variants (\"Botafogo - RJ\", \"Botafogo-RJ\", \"Botafogo RJ\"), accent variants (\"Ceara\"/\"Ceará\"), and full names (\"Fortaleza EC\" = \"Fortaleza\").\n\nBut there are tricky cases where the same base name maps to DIFFERENT clubs:\n- \"Botafogo - PB\" vs \"Botafogo - RJ\" → different clubs (Botafogo-PB vs Botafogo-RJ)\n- \"Botafogo SP\" → Botafogo-SP (Ribeirão Preto, SP) — different from Botafogo-RJ!\n- \"Santa Cruz - PE\" vs \"Santa Cruz - RN\" vs \"Santa Cruz - RS\" → different\n- \"América - MG\" vs \"América - RN\" → different\n- \"Vitória - BA\" vs \"Vitória ES\" (Vitória-ES) → different\n- \"Ypiranga - AP\" vs \"Ypiranga - RS\" → different\n- \"Guarani - CE\" vs \"Guarani - SP\" → different\n- \"Internacional - RS\" vs \"Internacional - SC\" → different (Inter de Lages?)\n- \"Atlético - AC/BA/GO/PR/MG\" → different clubs\n- \"Rio Branco - AC\" vs \"Rio Branco - ES\" → different\n- \"São Raimundo - PA\" vs \"São Raimundo - RR\" vs \"Sao Raimundo AM\" → different\n- \"Brasil - RS\" = Brasil de Pelotas\n- \"Athletico Paranaense\" = \"Athletico-PR\" = \"Atletico-PR\" = \"Atlético Paranaense\" = \"Atlético - PR\"\n- \"Atletico Mineiro\" = \"Atlético Mineiro - MG\" = \"Atletico-MG\" = \"Atlético-MG\" = \"Atlético - MG\"\n- \"Flamengo - PI\" vs \"Flamengo-RJ\" → different\n- \"Juventude - MA\" vs \"Juventude - RS\" → different\n\nSo the canonical identity MUST include the state suffix when present in the raw data. The rule:\n- Parse raw name → (base_name, state) where state = trailing \"-XX\"/\" - XX\"/\" XX\" (2-letter uppercase BR state code) or parenthesized country code for Libertadores.\n- Canonical key = normalize(base) + \"|\" + state IF the base name alone is ambiguous among clubs in the dataset (e.g., \"Botafogo\", \"Santa Cruz\", \"América\", \"Atlético\"...).\n\nRather than hardcoding ambiguity detection at load time, I can compute it: build registry of (base_key, state) → club. If a base_key maps to exactly one state across the entire dataset (considering all files), then state is redundant for that base → canonical key = base_key. If base_key maps to multiple states, then the club's canonical key = base_key + \"|\" + state.\n\nWait, but \"América - MG\" appears as \"América-MG\", \"America MG\", \"America - MG\", \"América - MG\", \"America-MG\". Also there's \"America FC Natal\" (without state!) and \"América de Natal - RN\" and \"América-RN\" and \"America RN\". So América-MG and América-RN: base \"america\" maps to states {MG, RN} → both get state-qualified keys. Good. And \"America FC Natal\" (a variant appearing in cup data?) hmm — \"America FC Natal\" base is \"america fc natal\" which is distinct base key from \"america\". That would create a SEPARATE club. Bad.\n\nSo pure-computational canonicalization is insufficient; I need a curated alias table for known variants. But I can combine: curated aliases for the well-known big clubs + generic state-suffix parsing. Let me design:\n\n1. `clean_team_name(raw)`: strip whitespace, strip parenthetical annotations (e.g., \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\"), strip country codes like \"(URU)\", \"(EQU)\", \"(PAR)\"? Let me check Libertadores names for country codes.\n\n2. `extract_state(raw)`: trailing pattern `(?:^|\\s)-\\s*([A-Z]{2})$` or `\\s([A-Z]{2})$` (e.g., \"ASA AL\", \"America MG\", \"Cuiaba MT\", \"Botafogo PB\", \"Vitoria ES\", \"URT MG\", \"Sao Jose RS\", \"CENE MS\", \"Operario MS\"). But careful: \"FC Cascavel\" ends with a word, fine. \"Rio Branco AC\" → state AC. \"ASA AL\" → base ASA + AL... ASA is a club in Arapiraca-AL; \"ASA\" alone also exists. OK.\n\nDanger: some names end with 2-letter non-state words? e.g. \"Real FC\" ends \"FC\" — not a state code. \"Aguia - PA\" fine. What about \"Boavista RJ\"? state RJ. Hmm \"Duque De Caxias RJ\" → RJ. What about \"Gremio RS\" (Grêmio) vs \"Gremio Anapolis\" — fine. \"Santos - AP\" (Santos-AP, Amapá club) vs \"Santos\" (SP club). Good thing we parse state.\n\nEdge: \"Juventude - MA\" — actually there's no Juventude-MA in Série C? There is (Juventude de São Luís? no that's Sampaio/Moto). Hmm, there might be a \"Juventude MA\" in data. Whatever — data-driven parsing works.\n\nEdge: \"River Plate - SE\" — a Brazilian club \"River Plate-SE\"? Actually \"River-PI\" (River do Piauí) and \"River AC\" (River-PI?) hmm \"River AC\" = River Esporte Clube? River is from Teresina-PI. \"River AC\" — River Atlético Clube is from Piauí (often styled River-PI). Hmm, \"River AC\" might be Ríver AC (Piauí). And \"River (PI)\". And \"Ríver - PI\". So variants: \"River - PI\", \"River (PI)\", \"River AC\", \"Ríver - PI\" all = Ríver (PI). I'll add curated alias: \"river ac\" → river|PI.\n\n3. Curated ALIAS map (normalized full raw → canonical display + state) for the important/big clubs to catch full-name variants that pure parsing misses:\n   - \"athletico paranaense\" → Atlético-PR display \"Athletico-PR\"? or \"Athletico Paranaense\"? I'll use display \"Athletico Paranaense\"? Hmm. Common canonical naming: \"Athletico-PR\". For display I'd like friendly names: \"Atlético-PR\"? The datasets use \"Athletico-PR\" and \"Atletico Paranaense\". I'll pick curated display names for the big ~30 clubs: Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Santos, Grêmio, Internacional, Cruzeiro, Atlético Mineiro (display \"Atlético-MG\"), Athletico Paranaense (display \"Athletico-PR\"), Botafogo, Fluminense, Vasco da Gama, Bahia, Fortaleza, Sport Recife, Coritiba, Athletico..., Ceará, Goiás, Vitória, Athletico-GO (\"Atlético Goianiense\" = \"Atletico Goianiense\" = \"Atlético-GO\"), América-MG, América-RN, Chapecoense, Cuiabá, Athletico Paranaense, Ponte Preta, Paraná, Sport, Athletico...\n\n   For non-ambiguous bases, display = cleaned base name (accented form preferred). For ambiguous bases (multi-state), display = cleaned base + \"-\" + state.\n\n4. FIFA club names: need to check what Brazilian clubs look like there: probably \"Flamengo\", \"Palmeiras\", \"São Paulo\", \"Santos\", \"Grêmio\", \"Internacional\", \"Corinthians\", \"Cruzeiro\", \"Atlético Mineiro\", \"Athletico Paranaense\", \"Botafogo\", \"Fluminense\", \"Vasco da Gama\", \"Bahia\", \"Sport Club Recife\", \"Ceará Sporting Club\", \"Fortaleza\", \"Goiás EC\", \"Chapecoense\"... So cross-file linking uses the same normalization + curated aliases. Let me dump the FIFA clubs of Brazilian players (club where nationality=Brazil) — that lists all Brazilian clubs in FIFA data.\n\nLet me check Libertadores team name formats (country codes) and FIFA clubs now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ad97b1a001hGCqhD8XWPmBLS","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","messageID":"msg_05ad86a910014ejOgArHiF7ykR"}}
