{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788230790374,"sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_7782fb7b9ff042f791c3c666","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', newline='', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    fifa = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nclubs = sorted({r['Club'] for r in fifa})\nfor probe in ['Flamengo','Palmeiras','Paulo','Corinthians','Vasco','Recife','Sporting','Gama','Mineiro','Braga','Sport']:\n    hits = [c for c in clubs if probe.lower() in c.lower()]\n    print(probe, \"->\", hits[:6])\nprint(len(clubs), \"total clubs\")\n# Gabriel Barbosa check\ngb = [r for r in fifa if 'gabriel' in r['Name'].lower() and 'barbosa' in r['Name'].lower()]\nprint(\"Gabriel Barbosa:\", [(r['Name'], r['Club'], r['Overall']) for r in gb])\n# Neymar\nnm = [r for r in fifa if 'neymar' in r['Name'].lower()]\nprint(\"Neymar:\", [(r['Name'], r['Nationality'], r['Overall'], r['Club']) for r in nm])\nEOF"},"output":"Flamengo -> []\nPalmeiras -> []\nPaulo -> []\nCorinthians -> []\nVasco -> []\nRecife -> ['Sport Club do Recife']\nSporting -> ['Ceará Sporting Club', 'Real Sporting de Gijón', 'Sporting CP', 'Sporting Kansas City', 'Sporting Lokeren', 'Sporting de Charleroi']\nGama -> []\nMineiro -> ['Atlético Mineiro']\nBraga -> ['SC Braga']\nSport -> ['Ceará Sporting Club', 'Clube Sport Marítimo', 'Real Sporting de Gijón', 'Sport Club do Recife', 'Sporting CP', 'Sporting Kansas City']\n652 total clubs\nGabriel Barbosa: []\nNeymar: [('Neymar Jr', 'Brazil', '92', 'Paris Saint-Germain')]\n","metadata":{"output":"Flamengo -> []\nPalmeiras -> []\nPaulo -> []\nCorinthians -> []\nVasco -> []\nRecife -> ['Sport Club do Recife']\nSporting -> ['Ceará Sporting Club', 'Real Sporting de Gijón', 'Sporting CP', 'Sporting Kansas City', 'Sporting Lokeren', 'Sporting de Charleroi']\nGama -> []\nMineiro -> ['Atlético Mineiro']\nBraga -> ['SC Braga']\nSport -> ['Ceará Sporting Club', 'Clube Sport Marítimo', 'Real Sporting de Gijón', 'Sport Club do Recife', 'Sporting CP', 'Sporting Kansas City']\n652 total clubs\nGabriel Barbosa: []\nNeymar: [('Neymar Jr', 'Brazil', '92', 'Paris Saint-Germain')]\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', newline='', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    fifa = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nclubs = sorted({r['Club'] for r in fifa})\nfor probe in ['Flamengo','Palmeiras','Paulo','Corinthians','Vasco','Recife','Sporting','Gama','Mineiro','Braga','Sport']:\n    hits = [c for c in clubs if probe.lower() in c.lower()]\n    print(probe, \"->\", hits[:6])\nprint(len(clubs), \"total clubs\")\n# Gabriel Barbosa check\ngb = [r for r in fifa if 'gabriel' in r['Name'].lower() and 'barbosa' in r['Name'].lower()]\nprint(\"Gabriel Barbosa:\", [(r['Name'], r['Club'], r['Overall']) for r in gb])\n# Neymar\nnm = [r for r in fifa if 'neymar' in r['Name'].lower()]\nprint(\"Neymar:\", [(r['Name'], r['Nationality'], r['Overall'], r['Club']) for r in nm])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788230790357,"end":1788230790373}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have the full picture. Key cross-file linking cases:\n\nFIFA Brazilian clubs → match-data canonical names:\n- \"Atlético Mineiro\" → Atlético-MG\n- \"Atlético Paranaense\" → Athletico-PR (match data uses \"Athletico-PR\", \"Athletico Paranaense\", \"Atletico-PR\", \"Atlético - PR\"...)\n- \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → América-MG\n- \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → Ceará\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\" → Sport\n- \"Santos\" → Santos (also \"Santos Laguna\" is Mexican — different; and match data has \"Santos - AP\" (Amapá)!)\n- \"Vitória\" → Vitória (BA). But \"Vitória Guimarães\"/\"Vitória de Setúbal\" are Portuguese — distinct names, fine.\n- \"Vasco\" in Libertadores = Vasco da Gama.\n- Libertadores country-code suffixes: \"Guaraní (PAR)\" / \"Guaraní-PAR\", \"Nacional (URU)\" / \"Nacional-URU\", \"River Plate-URU\" vs \"River Plate\" (Argentina), \"Universitario-PER\" vs \"Universitario de Sucre\" (Bolivia) vs \"Universidad...\" etc. So for Libertadores, country codes MUST be part of identity: \"Nacional (PAR)\" vs \"Nacional (URU)\" are different clubs. Pattern: trailing 2-3 letter country code in parens or after hyphen: PAR, URU, EQU, PER, VEN, ARG, BOL, CHI, COL, MEX... e.g. \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Delfín-EQU\", \"Olimpia-PAR\", \"Libertad-PAR\", \"Trujillanos (VEN)\", \"Universitario (PER)\". Note \"River Plate\" (no code) = Argentina; \"River Plate-URU\" = Uruguay's Danubio? no, River Plate Montevideo. So treat trailing country code as part of identity: canonical = base + \" (\" + code + \")\" when code present, else base. But careful: BR state codes are also 2-letter uppercase, and Brazilian clubs from Libertadores have no suffix. So parse trailing paren/hyphen code as \"country code\" generically, keep it in identity.\n\n  BUT the BR match files also use hyphenated state codes: \"Palmeiras-SP\". Ugh — same pattern! Distinguish by known set: BR state codes = {AC,AL,AP,AM,BA,CE,DF,ES,GO,MA,MT,MS,MG,PA,PB,PR,PE,PI,RJ,RN,RS,RO,RR,SC,SP,SE,TO}. Non-state codes in Libertadores: URU, PAR, EQU, PER, VEN, MEX, BOL? codes are 2-3 letters. Rule: trailing code in parens OR after hyphen/space:\n  - if code ∈ BR_STATES → it's a state suffix (keep as identity component)\n  - else → country suffix (keep as identity component)\n  Either way, keep the suffix in the canonical key. Then the ambiguity resolution (drop suffix only when base is globally unambiguous) can handle display.\n\n  Wait — actually for identity purposes, my earlier plan was: canonical key = base + state if base is ambiguous. With Libertadores codes, \"Nacional (PAR)\" base \"nacional\" also appears as \"Atlético Nacional\" (different base) and \"Nacional AM\" (Brazilian, cup data — \"Nacional - AM\", \"Nacional AM\"). So base \"nacional\" maps to PAR, URU, AM → ambiguous → keys \"nacional|PAR\", \"nacional|URU\", \"nacional|AM\". \n\n  For \"River Plate\" (ARG) vs \"River Plate-URU\": base \"river plate\" → {ARG(no code), URU} → ambiguous → keys differ. Good.\n\n  For \"Universitario (PER)\" vs \"Universitario de Sucre\" vs \"Universitario-PER\": bases \"universitario\" (PER x2 forms unify) and \"universitario de sucre\". Good.\n\n  BUT: \"San José\" (Bolivia) — fine. \"Colón\" — fine.\n\n  Tricky: \"Real Atlético\" hmm whatever.\n\n  Also: \"Athletico\" (bare) in Libertadores = Athletico-PR. Alias: \"athletico\" → athletico|PR. And \"Atlético Nacional\" is Colombia. OK.\n\nNow the base-ambiguity logic needs the suffix to be part of key computation. Plan for canonicalization:\n\n```\ndef parse_team(raw):\n    raw = raw.strip()\n    # strip annotations in parens that are not country codes, e.g. \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\"\n    # extract trailing code: r'[,\\s]*[-–\\s]\\s*\\(?([A-Z]{2,3})\\)?\\s*$' — hmm also \"ASA AL\" (space separated), \"America MG\", \"Cuiaba MT\", \"URT MG\", \"CENE MS\", \"Boavista RJ\", \"Vitoria ES\"\n    # Also \"River (PI)\" pattern: code inside parens at end.\n```\n\nPatterns to handle:\n1. \"Palmeiras-SP\" → base Palmeiras, state SP\n2. \"Atlético - MG\" → base Atlético, state MG\n3. \"America MG\" → base America, state MG\n4. \"River (PI)\" → base River, state PI\n5. \"Nacional (URU)\" → base Nacional, country URU\n6. \"Barcelona-EQU\" → base Barcelona, country EQU\n7. \"ASA AL\" → base ASA, state AL\n8. \"CENE MS\" → base CENE, state MS\n9. \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → strip paren annotation → base Boavista Sport Club, state RJ\n10. \"Rentistas \" (trailing space) → base Rentistas\n11. \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" → base \"Vasco Da Gama\", state RJ\n\nTrailing code regex: `\\s*[\\(\\[]?\\s*[-–—]?\\s*([A-Z]{2,3})\\s*[\\)\\]]?\\s*$` — but must not match regular words that happen to be 2-3 uppercase letters like \"CENE\" (CENE is base + state MS — fine, matches \"CENE MS\"), or \"URT MG\", \"URT\" alone (base URT), \"CRB\" alone (base), \"CSA\" alone, \"ABC\" alone, \"PSTC\" alone, \"PSTC - PR\". Words like \"ABC\" — a trailing-code regex on \"ABC\" would see \"ABC\" itself as potential code but there's no separator; require separator OR paren when the token precedes. Actually for \"ASA AL\", separator is a space. For \"ABC\" alone, the regex would match \"ABC\" as code with empty base — require base non-empty. \"CRB\" alone: base CRB. But wait — would `\\s+([A-Z]{2,3})$` on \"CSA\" strip \"SA\"? No — regex requires the whole trailing token: `(?P<base>.+?)\\s*[-–—\\s]+\\(?([A-Z]{2,3})\\)?$` with base non-greedy... Let me think: on \"CSA\", a regex `\\b([A-Z]{2,3})$` would match \"CSA\" (3 uppercase letters) leaving base \"CS\"?? If base must be non-empty, it would strip \"A\" leaving \"CS\" — WRONG.\n\nFix: only treat trailing code as suffix if separated by: space+code (2 letters only? \"CENE MS\" state MS is 2; \"ASA AL\" 2; but \"America MG\" — MG 2 letters), hyphen+code, or parenthesized code. And critically, the code must be exactly 2 or 3 letters ALL-CAPS AND (base must contain a space or the code is preceded by separator)... Hmm \"ASA AL\": base \"ASA\" code \"AL\". \"CSA\": no separator → whole thing is base. \"ABC\": base. \"CRB\": base. \"URT\": base. \"Gama\": base.\n\nRule: trailing token (after whitespace/hyphen split) matches ^[A-Z]{2,3}$ AND is in the known-code set (BR states ∪ country codes {URU, PAR, EQU, PER, VEN, MEX, BOL, ARG, CHI, COL, USA?, TRI?...}). With known-code set, \"ABC\" (not a code) stays base. \"ASA AL\" → AL is BR state → base ASA. \"CENE MS\" → MS state → base CENE. \n\nEdge: \"Nautico-PE\" → PE state. \"Flamengo-RJ\" → RJ. \"Cuiaba MT\" → MT. \"Cuiaba-MT\". Good. What about names like \"Rentistas \" → strip. \"Montevideo Wanderers\" → base. \"San José\" → base.\n\nWhat about \"Juventude - MA\"? Juventude... there IS \"Juventude MA\"? Hmm the list shows 'Juventude', 'Juventude - MA', 'Juventude MA', 'Juventude-RS'. \"Juventude - MA\" — is that a real different club (Juventude de São Luís?) Hmm, actually there's \"Juventude Samay\"? Whatever — data-driven: base \"juventude\" maps to states {MA, RS} → ambiguous → keys juventude|MA, juventude|RS. It's consistent; even if both were the same real-world club, consistency is preserved... but would be wrong to say they're different clubs. Juventude-MA exists (Esporte Clube Juventude de São Luís? no...). There is \"Juventude MA\" hmm. I'll accept data-driven ambiguity; it's consistent within the dataset.\n\nBUT there's a catch with ambiguity + missing suffix: e.g. \"Santos\" (Brasileirão, no suffix) and \"Santos-SP\" (novo/BR-Football \"Santos\") and cup \"Santos - SP\"? and Libertadores \"Santos\" (no state) — the Brazilian club Santos-SP. Plus \"Santos - AP\" (Amapá club in Copa do Brasil). Base \"santos\" → states {SP, AP, NONE(Libertadores)}. The NONE state is the problem: Libertadores \"Santos\" has no suffix but is the same club as \"Santos-SP\".\n\nResolution: when base is ambiguous, entries WITHOUT a suffix need resolution. Approach: a curated DEFAULT_STATE / alias map for famous clubs whose bare name = a specific state-qualified club:\n- santos → SP (Santos FC)\n- vasco → RJ (Vasco da Gama; \"Vasco\" bare in Libertadores)\n- america → MG? NO — \"America - MG\" always suffixed in match data; bare \"America\"? not in list. Skip.\n- athletico → PR (Libertadores bare \"Athletico\")\n- atletico mineiro → MG; atletico paranaense → PR; atletico goianiense → GO\n- river → PI (\"River AC\", \"River (PI)\", \"Ríver - PI\")\n- vitoria → BA (bare \"Vitoria\" in FIFA-linked? \"Vitória\" in match data is suffixed? list shows 'Vitoria', 'Vitória', 'Vitória - BA', 'Vitoria-BA', 'Vitoria ES', 'Vitória da Conquista'. Bare \"Vitoria\"/\"Vitória\" → BA. \"Vitoria ES\" suffixed ES → Vitória-ES. Good.)\n- internacional → RS (bare \"Internacional\" common; \"Internacional - SC\" → Internacional-SC (Lages)). Base \"internacional\" states {RS, SC, NONE} → ambiguous → bare → RS by default.\n- bahia → BA (bare \"Bahia\" = EC Bahia; \"Bahia de Feira\" is different base; \"EC Bahia\" alias → bahia|BA? Hmm \"EC Bahia\" base \"ec bahia\" — alias to Bahia.)\n- Also base \"brazil\"? fine.\n- \"Cuiaba\" bare → MT (\"Cuiaba\", \"Cuiaba MT\", \"Cuiaba-MT\", \"Cuiabá - MT\") — base cuiaba states {MT, NONE} → not ambiguous... it maps to {MT} only plus NONE. Rule refinement: NONE entries collapse to the majority/explicit state when the base has exactly ONE state code overall! If base maps to exactly one state (some rows suffixed, some not), then bare and suffixed = same club → key = base alone (drop state entirely). That handles Santos? No — Santos has {SP, AP} → ambiguous. So Santos needs the default-state alias. Cuiabá: only MT → unify as \"Cuiabá\". \n\nSo algorithm:\n1. Parse (base, code) where code = state/country or None.\n2. Alias map applied on normalized base+code BEFORE ambiguity: curated corrections like \"america fc natal\" → base america code RN; \"america de natal\" → RN; \"athletico\" (bare) → PR; \"atletico goianiense\" → GO; \"atletico mineiro\" → MG; \"athletico paranaense\"/\"atletico paranaense\" → PR; \"vasco da gama\" → RJ; \"ec bahia\" → BA; \"river ac\" → PI; \"botafogo sp\" → SP?? careful \"Botafogo SP\" — is SP a state suffix or part of name \"Botafogo-SP\"? It IS part of the name (Botafogo Futebol Clube de Ribeirão Preto is commonly \"Botafogo-SP\"). If I parse SP as state → base botafogo code SP → same key as \"Botafogo - SP\"?? wait \"Botafogo SP\" vs \"Botafogo - SP\"... does \"Botafogo - SP\" exist? Not in the list. We have 'Botafogo - PB', 'Botafogo - RJ', 'Botafogo PB', 'Botafogo RJ', 'Botafogo-RJ', 'Botafogo SP'. So base \"botafogo\" → {PB, RJ, SP}: three clubs → keys botafogo|PB, botafogo|RJ, botafogo|SP. Display names: \"Botafogo-PB\", \"Botafogo-RJ\", \"Botafogo-SP\". \n3. After aliasing, group by base: if len(states incl. NONE... ) — treat: distinct codes = {codes of entries} where None counts. If set of codes (excluding None) has size 1 and None also present → all same club, key = base (display = accented base). If codes excluding None has size ≥ 2 → each None entry must be resolved: look in curated DEFAULT_CODE map (normalized base → code); if found → key = base|code; else → key stays base alone as its own club (unresolvable — keep separate). If codes size 1 and no None → key = base (drop state, since no conflict) — but wait: that would merge \"América-MG\" & \"América-RN\"? No — those have 2 distinct codes → ambiguous path. If size 1 (e.g. cuiaba only MT with bare forms) → key = base, display \"Cuiabá\". Good — this is the right simplification.\n4. Display names: prefer the most common accented raw form (or curated DISPLAY map for big clubs: e.g. base|MG \"Atlético-MG\"...). Simpler: display = accented base (from most common variant) plus \"-\" + code only when ambiguous: display = f\"{accented_base}-{code}\".\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: \"Atlético - AC\" (Atlético Acreano?) and \"Atlético - BA\" (Atlético de Alagoinhas?) — these exist as separate keys atlético|AC, atlético|BA. Fine.\n\nNow curated alias/base corrections needed (normalized base → base+code), for entries whose name doesn't parse cleanly or bare-ambiguous:\n- \"america fc natal\" → america + RN\n- \"america de natal - rn\" → parses already (base america de natal, code RN) → alias \"america de natal\" → \"america\"+RN\n- \"athletico\" bare → PR\n- \"atletico goianiense\" → GO\n- \"atletico mineiro\" / \"atlético mineiro\" → MG\n- \"athletico paranaense\" / \"atletico paranaense\" → PR\n- \"vasco da gama\" → RJ (from \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\"? that parses as base \"Vasco Da Gama\" code RJ → alias base \"vasco da gama\" → base vasco code RJ)\n- \"ec bahia\" → bahia+BA\n- \"river ac\" → river+PI\n- \"brasil rs\"? \"Brasil - RS\" parses → base brasil code RS; \"Brasil de Pelotas\" base \"brasil de pelotas\" → alias → brasil+RS\n- \"sao jose poa\"? \"Sao Jose - POA\" → POA is not in code set → base \"sao jose poa\"?? Hmm \"Sao Jose - POA\": code POA unknown → whole stays base \"Sao Jose - POA\". Alias → \"sao jose\" + RS. (\"Sao Jose RS\" exists, \"São José - RS\" exists.) Yes add.\n- \"desportiva ferroviaria\" → desportiva+ES (\"Desportiva - ES\", \"Desportiva Ferroviaria - ES\", hmm base \"desportiva ferroviaria\" code ES → alias base → \"desportiva\"+ES)\n- \"america fc natal\" covered.\n- \"boavista sc saquarema\"? \"Boavista SC Saquarema\" vs \"Boavista Sport Club (...)\" vs \"Boavista\" — all Boavista-RJ? \"Boavista - RJ\", \"Boavista RJ\", \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo...)\", \"Boavista SC Saquarema\". Also FIFA \"Boavista FC\" (Portuguese club!) — different, but FIFA linking only matters for Brazilian clubs; Boavista FC (POR) players are Portuguese. Eh — if a query asks \"players at Boavista\", ambiguous. Fine.\n- \"crac\"? \"C.r.a.c. - GO\" → base \"C.r.a.c.\" code GO; \"CRAC\" base; alias \"c.r.a.c.\" → crac. Add \"normalize periods away\": strip dots in base key? \"A.b.c. - RN\" vs \"ABC - RN\" vs \"Abc - RN\" → base normalization should strip punctuation/dots: \"a.b.c.\" → \"abc\". YES — canonical_key should remove all non-alphanumeric chars. Then \"A.b.c.\" → \"abc\" == \"ABC\". \n- \"cs alagoano\" vs \"CSA\" — CS Alagoano IS CSA? Centro Sportivo Alagoano = CSA. 'CS Alagoano' in list. Alias \"cs alagoano\" → csa. Hmm CSA base maps to {AL} only → key csa. OK.\n- \"crb\" / \"C.r.b. - AL\" → crb. dots stripped → \"crb\" ✓.\n- \"csa\"/\"Csa-AL\" ✓.\n- \"ser caxias - rs\" → SER Caxias = Sergipe? no — \"Ser Caxias\" is Sociedade Esportiva Recreativa Caxias do Sul = SER Caxias = \"Caxias\"! Alias \"ser caxias\" → caxias+RS. Hmm — Caxias do Sul club commonly \"Caxias\". 'Caxias', 'Caxias - RS', 'Caxias RS', 'Ser Caxias - RS' all → caxias|RS.\n- \"grêmio prudente\"? \"Grêmio Prudente\" distinct from Grêmio. Leave.\n- \"nautico capibaribe\" → nautico+PE.\n- \"operario ferroviario esporte c - pr\" → operario+PR (\"Operario - PR\", \"Oper