ng. If BR-Football Serie A covers 2013-2023, dedupe (season, home, away) across sources handles it.\n\n  Dedupe keeps \"each match appears once\" → stats correct, H2H lists clean. Matches carry `sources` list? Single source (the kept one). Add `datasets` field listing which source files contained it (nice provenance, cheap to track).\n\n  Hmm, careful with dedupe across DIFFERENT competitions named Serie A but different sources — that's exactly the point (same real competition). But \"Copa do Brasil\" from BR-Football + Brazilian_Cup_Matches — same competition ✓. What about BR-Football \"Serie B\" vs nothing else (only source) ✓.\n\n  One more wrinkle: Brasileirão Série A 2023 (BR-Football) vs Brasileirao_Matches which ends 2022 → no overlap there; fine.\n\n**Dates:** parse datetime strings; store `date` (ISO) and optional `time`. Formats: \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\", \"2023-09-24\", \"29/03/2003\". BR-Football has separate date + time columns (\"20:00:00\").\n\n**MCP tools (final list):**\n1. `search_matches` (team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit=25) → matches + h2h summary when both teams given.\n2. `get_head_to_head` (team_a, team_b, competition=None) → record + matches.\n3. `get_team_stats` (team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None) → W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate, home/away split.\n4. `get_standings` (competition, season, top=None) → table.\n5. `search_players` (name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=25) → players sorted by overall desc.\n6. `get_player` (name) → best match details incl. skills subset.\n7. `get_competitions` () → catalog with seasons + match counts.\n8. `get_statistics` (competition=None, season=None) → avg goals, home win rate, biggest wins, best home/away teams, totals.\n9. `get_derbies` (season=None, competition=None) → derby matches grouped by rivalry.\n10. `get_team_history` (team, limit?) → competitions played, seasons, overall record. (covers \"What competitions has Palmeiras played in?\")\n11. `natural_language_query` (question) → keyword router to above tools (best-effort, for demo integration with LLM).\n\nResources: `soccer://status` (dataset summary), `soccer://teams`, `soccer://competitions`, `soccer://derbies`.\n\n**Server:** FastMCP(name=\"brazilian-soccer\"), tools registered; run stdio by default, `--transport sse --port` optional? Keep stdio only + env var? Simple: stdio (MCP default) + document. Add `--http` optional maybe not needed. Keep stdio; plus allow SSE? FastMCP supports .run(transport='stdio'). I'll do stdio only, clean.\n\n**Loading:** lazy at startup (module-level singleton created on first tool call or at server init). For tests, create store directly with explicit data dir.\n\n**Performance:** loading ~34k rows via csv — fast (<1.5s). Lookups O(1)-ish via indexes. Standings computed per call but cheap (6.8k rows max). Fine.\n\nNow — test count: \"At least 20 sample questions can be answered\" — I'll write BDD scenarios covering 20+ questions from the spec, plus a checklist test that runs all 20 sample questions against the store and asserts non-empty/valid results.\n\nLet me also check BR-Football years and the fifa columns I need (weight/height parse). And check `novo` \"OBS\" column (empty?). And confirm cup \"round\" strings are numbers.\n\nOne more check: BR-Football date range per tournament, and whether goals can be NaN.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ade4544001NDykVIQwKsBwh3","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","messageID":"msg_05adc6e2300171EEFY4qDiyZZm"}}
