sc\"... let me spell: \"ceara\"+\"sporting\"+\"club\" = \"cearasc\" — hmm, \"ceara\" then \"sporting\" gives \"cearasc\"? c,e,a,r,a,s,p,o... that's \"cearasc\" — no! \"ceara\"+\"sporting\" = \"cearasc\"?? c-e-a-r-a + s-p-o-r-t-i-n-g = \"cearasc\"... I'm confusing myself. \"ceara\"+\"sporting\" concatenated = \"cearasc\"? No!! It's \"cearasc\" — ugh, concatenation of \"ceara\" and \"sporting\" is literally \"cearasc\"... \n\nNO. Concatenate the strings: \"ceara\" = c,e,a,r,a. \"sporting\" = s,p,o,r,t,i,n,g. \"ceara\"+\"sporting\" = \"cearasc\"?? The first 5 chars are \"ceara\", then \"sporting\": \"cearasporting\". Then +\"club\" = \"cearasportingclub\". So canonical_key(\"Ceará Sporting Club\") = \"cearasportingclub\". My alias key must be \"cearasportingclub\". Ugh — and the duplicated \"cearasc\" lines are garbage. Let me carefully rewrite that whole alias section computing keys mentally:\n\n- \"América FC Natal\" → tokens [america, fc, natal] → strip leading \"fc\"? No — leading token is \"america\", trailing \"natal\". No stripping. → \"americafcnatal\" ✓ (as written)\n- \"América de Natal\" → \"americadenatal\" ✓\n- \"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"atleticomineiro\" ✓\n- \"Atletico Goianiense\" → \"atleticogoianiense\" ✓\n- \"Athletico Paranaense\" → \"athleticoparanaense\" ✓\n- \"Atletico Paranaense\" → \"atleticoparanaense\" ✓\n- \"Athletico\" → \"athletico\" ✓\n- \"CA Parana\" → tokens [ca, parana] → leading \"ca\" stripped → \"parana\" — alias \"caparana\" dead, remove.\n- \"Vasco da Gama\" → \"vascodagama\" ✓\n- \"Sport Club do Recife\" → tokens [sport, club, do, recife] → no edge tokens (\"sport\" not in _CLUB_TOKENS, \"recife\" no) → \"sportclubdorecife\" ✓\n- \"Sport Recife\" → \"sportrecife\" ✓\n- \"Fortaleza FC\" → trailing \"fc\" stripped → \"fortaleza\" — alias \"fortalezafc\" dead. Remove both fortaleza aliases? \"Fortaleza EC\" → [fortaleza, ec] → trailing stripped → \"fortaleza\" ✓ dead too. Remove.\n- \"EC Bahia\" → leading \"ec\" stripped → \"bahia\" ✓ dead alias, remove.\n- \"EC Internacional SC\" → [ec, internacional, sc] → both edges stripped → \"internacional\" — dead alias. And \"Internacional SC\" → [internacional, sc] → trailing \"sc\" stripped → \"internacional\" — dead. REMOVE both! Wait — that changes behavior: \"Internacional - SC\" parses → base \"Internacional\" code SC (via regex, before canonical) → identity (internacional, SC) ✓ good. But \"EC Internacional SC\" (no hyphen) → canonical strips \"sc\" as token → base_key \"internacional\", code None → becomes bare → DEFAULT_STATE → internacional|RS ✗ WRONG (it's Internacional-SC). Hmm! The token-stripping of \"sc\" hurts here. Tricky: \"EC Internacional SC\" means EC Internacional (Santa Maria? no — Inter de Lages is \"Internacional-SC\"). \"EC Internacional SC\" = Esporte Clube Internacional (Lages/SC). With code lost, alias must restore it: alias \"ecinternacionalsc\"? — but canonical ALREADY stripped tokens, so lookup key is \"internacional\" → can't distinguish from plain Internacional. DAMN.\n  \n  How often does \"EC Internacional SC\" appear? It was in the raw name list. If I alias \"internacional\" → (\"internacional\", None) — no help. Options: (a) special-case: in register(), check the RAW for patterns like \"Internacional SC\" before canonicalization... (b) accept the merge into Internacional-RS (wrong for that small club, appears in Copa do Brasil early rounds probably). Given demo scope, (b) is acceptable BUT let me instead handle in parse: treat \"Internacional SC\" style where the FINAL token \"SC\" is a state code of a club in SC... \n  \n  Alternative cleaner approach: in canonical_key, DON'T strip trailing club tokens when the stripped token was already consumed as a state code... no, \"EC Internacional SC\" has SC not parsed as code (no separator? there IS a space: \"Internacional SC\" — regex WOULD parse code SC! \"EC Internacional SC\": regex → base \"EC Internacional\", code \"SC\" ✓✓✓. The regex runs FIRST on the raw string with case-sensitive [A-Z]{2,3} — \"SC\" matches! So parse_team_name(\"EC Internacional SC\") → base \"EC Internacional\", code SC ✓. Then canonical_key(\"EC Internacional\") strips \"ec\" → \"internacional\" → variant (internacional, SC) ✓ CORRECT — no alias needed!\n  \n  Wait then why did I think SC was lost? Because in my earlier mental trace I applied canonical_key to the WHOLE raw. No — parse_team_name is applied to raw first, extracting code. The regex needs the code at END of raw. \"EC Internacional SC\" ends with \"SC\" ✓. So dead aliases, remove. Similarly \"Ceará Sporting Club\": no trailing code → base \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → canonical \"cearasportingclub\" → alias \"cearasportingclub\" → (\"ceara\", None) ✓.\n  \n- \"Ceará Sporting Club\" → alias \"cearasportingclub\" ✓ (fix key!)\n- \"Brasil de Pelotas\" → \"brasildepelotas\" ✓\n- \"Sao Jose - POA\" → parse: trailing \"POA\" — not in CODES (POA not a state!) → regex match rejected (code not in CODES) → base \"Sao Jose - POA\" full → canonical \"saojosepoa\" → alias → (\"saojose\",\"RS\") ✓ (key correct as written)\n- \"Desportiva Ferroviaria\" → \"desportivaferroviaria\" ✓ (also \"Desportiva Ferroviaria - ES\" parses code ES → base \"Desportiva Ferroviaria\" → alias → desportiva+ES ✓)\n- \"SER Caxias\" → \"sercaxias\" ✓\n- \"CS Alagoano\" → \"csalagoano\" ✓\n- \"Operario Ferroviario Esporte C - PR\" → regex: code PR ✓ base \"Operario Ferroviario Esporte C\" → canonical: tokens [operario, ferroviario, esporte, c] → trailing \"c\" not a token → \"operarioferroviarioesportec\" ✓ as written\n- \"Boavista SC Saquarema\" → \"boavistscsaquarema\"? No: [boavista, sc, saquarema] — \"sc\" is MIDDLE token, not stripped → \"boavistascsaquarema\". Hmm wait: tokens = boavista, sc, saquarema. Leading \"boavista\" not token; trailing \"saquarema\" not token. Join → \"boavistasc\"+\"saquarema\" = \"boavistascsaquarema\"? Concatenate: \"boavista\"+\"sc\"+\"saquarema\" = \"boavistascsaquarema\" ✓ (as written — correct!)\n- \"Boavista Sport Club\" → [boavista, sport, club] → \"boavistasportclub\" ✓ as written. (And raw \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo...) - RJ\" → annotation removed → \"Boavista Sport Club  - RJ\" → base \"Boavista Sport Club\" code RJ → alias → boavista+RJ ✓.)\n- \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → annotation stripped → \"América FC\" → canonical \"america\" → DEFAULT_STATE MG ✓ — alias \"americafcminasgerais\" dead, remove.\n- \"River AC\" → regex: trailing \"AC\" is a BR state → base \"River\" code AC?!! Ríver is from PIAUÍ not Acre! Regex parses \"AC\" as state AC → variant (river, AC) — WRONG. Alias can't fire (base_key \"river\" has no alias; alias key \"riverac\" never computed). FIX: alias on \"river\"?? But \"River - PI\"/\"River (PI)\"/\"Ríver - PI\" → base \"River\"/\"Ríver\" code PI ✓. Only \"River AC\" is problematic. Solution: alias (river, AC) → PI? My alias maps base_key → (base, code): entry \"river\": (\"river\",\"PI\")?? That would force ALL river variants to PI ✓ (River is only Ríver-PI in this dataset — \"River Plate\" has different base). Add alias \"river\": (\"river\",\"PI\"). But careful: alias forced code overrides parsed code — \"River - PI\" → base_key \"river\" → alias (\"river\",\"PI\") ✓ same result. ✓\n- \"Ríver - PI\" ✓ same.\n- \"Águia de Marabá\" → \"aguiademaraba\" ✓\n- \"Iv de Julho - PI\" → base \"Iv de Julho\" code PI → \"ivdejulho\" ✓\n- \"Clube Do Remo\" → \"clubederemo\" ✓\n- \"Nautico Capibaribe\" → \"nauticocapibaribe\" ✓\n- \"SE Gama\" → tokens [se, gama] → leading \"se\" not in tokens... \"se\" IS not in _CLUB_TOKENS — hmm \"SE Gama\" → \"segama\" ✓ alias as written.\n- \"Macae Esporte FC\" → trailing fc stripped → \"macaesporte\" — alias \"macaesportefc\" dead, remove.\n- \"Deportes Tolima\" → \"deportestolima\" ✓\n- \"Red Bull Brasil\" → \"redbullbrasil\" ✓\n- \"Botafogo SP FC\"? doesn't exist. \"Botafogo SP\" → regex trailing \"SP\" → base \"Botafogo\" code SP ✓. Dead alias \"botafogospfc\" remove.\n- \"Duque de Caxias FC\" → trailing fc → \"duquedecaxias\" — dead alias remove.\n\nAlso remove duplicates. Rewrite ALIASES section properly. And remove \"bragantino\" alias — wait I removed it already? In the rewrite I dropped \"bragantino\": bare \"Bragantino\" → DEFAULT_STATE[\"redbullbragantino\"]? No — base \"Bragantino\" → key \"bragantino\"; DEFAULT_STATE has \"redbullbragantino\" — mismatch! Bare \"Bragantino\" is Clube Atlético Bragantino (SP). \"Bragantino - PA\" → bragantino|PA. \"Bragantino - SP\" → bragantino|SP. Bare \"Bragantino\" → DEFAULT_STATE[\"bragantino\"]=\"SP\" needed. Also \"Red Bull Bragantino - SP\" → base \"Red Bull Bragantino\" → \"redbullbragantino\" code SP; \"Red Bull Brasil\" → alias → (\"redbullbragantino\",\"SP\") — hmm, that creates SEPARATE club \"redbullbragantino|SP\" vs \"bragantino|SP\"! Red Bull Bragantino IS Bragantino (renamed 2019). Merge: alias \"redbullbragantino\" → (\"bragantino\", \"SP\")?? But \"Red Bull Bragantino - SP\" base_key \"redbullbragantino\" → alias forces code SP ✓; \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" same ✓; \"Red Bull Brasil - SP\" → base_key \"redbullbrasil\" → alias (\"bragantino\",\"SP\")? I wrote alias \"redbullbrasil\" → (\"redbullbragantino\",\"SP\") — change to (\"bragantino\",\"SP\"). And \"Bragantino\" bare → default SP ✓ via DEFAULT_STATE[\"bragantino\"]. And \"Red Bull Brasil\" bare (no code) → alias (\"bragantino\",\"SP\") ✓.\n  So: ALIASES[\"redbullbragantino\"] = (\"bragantino\",\"SP\"); ALIASES[\"redbullbrasil\"] = (\"bragantino\",\"SP\"); ALIASES[\"bragantinosp\"] = (\"bragantino\",\"SP\")? \"Bragantino SP\" — regex parses code SP anyway ✓. DEFAULT_STATE[\"bragantino\"]=\"SP\" ✓ keep.\n\nAlso \"sao raimundo\": \"Sao Raimundo AM\" → regex: \"AM\" state → base \"Sao Raimundo\" code AM → (saoraimundo, AM); \"São Raimundo - PA\" → PA; \"Sao Raimundo RR\"/\"São Raimundo - RR\" → RR; bare? \"Sao Raimundo\" not bare... wait list has 'Sao Raimundo AM', 'Sao Raimundo PA', 'Sao Raimundo RR', 'São Raimundo - PA', 'São Raimundo - RR'. All coded! DEFAULT_STATE[\"saoraimundo\"] unnecessary but harmless. Keep (in case FIFA has bare \"São Raimundo\"? FIFA clubs list doesn't. fine).\n\nAlso check \"Nautico-PE\"/\"Náutico\"/\"Náutico - PE\"/\"Nautico Capibaribe\" — states {PE} → unify \"nautico\" ✓ default unneeded. Keep harmless.\n\nOne more: \"Rio Branco AC\" → regex base \"Rio Branco\" code AC ✓. \"Rio Branco - AC\" ✓. \"Rio Branco ES\" → code ES → rio branco|ES separate ✓. \"Rio Branco - ES\" ✓.\n\n\"Galvez AC\" → code AC ✓ (Galvez is Acre club ✓ correct).\n\n\"Juventude - MA\"/\"Juventude MA\" — real club Juventude de São Luís? Hmm whatever, data-consistent.\n\nNow also the `key_for` post-finalize path duplicates the resolution logic — fine.\n\nLet me apply these fixes.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ae9bb620013pKbtjnyOokIt1","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","messageID":"msg_05ae7cc5e0017PlpcWxEd2AtrX"}}
