 RN\" → tokens [abc, rn] → trailing \"rn\" not a club token → \"abcrn\". My test expectation was wrong — canonical_key is applied to the BASE after parse_team_name splits the state. So the test case is invalid for the full string. Fix test: parse_team_name first then canonical_key, or just drop those cases. I'll fix the test cases:\n   - \"ABC - RN\" → parse_team_name gives base \"ABC\" code RN → canonical \"abc\" ✓ — test should use parse_team_name.\n   - \"Vitoria F. C.\" → tokens [vitoria, f, c] → trailing \"c\" not stripped → \"vitoriafc\"? Hmm — dots→spaces: \"Vitoria F. C.\" → \"vitoria f  c\" → tokens [vitoria, f, c] → \"vitoriafc\". Expected \"vitoria\" — need \"f c\" handling! \"Vitoria F. C.\" comes with code ES in real data: parse_team_name(\"Vitoria F. C. - ES\") → base \"Vitoria F. C.\" code ES → canonical_key(\"Vitoria F. C.\") = \"vitoriafc\". The real raw \"Vitoria F. C. - ES\" would key \"vitoriafc\" vs \"Vitoria\"/\"Vitória - BA\" → \"vitoria\" — SPLIT! Let me handle: strip dots by REPLACING with \"\" (no space): \"vitoria f. c.\" → replace \".\" with \"\" → \"vitoria f c\" → same issue. Better: remove dots entirely: \"Vitoria F. C.\" → \"vitoria fc\" → tokens [vitoria, fc] → trailing fc stripped → \"vitoria\" ✓✓. And \"A.b.c.\" → remove dots → \"abc\" ✓ (even better than before). \"C.r.b. - AL\" → parse base \"C.r.b.\" code AL → key \"crb\" ✓.\n   \n   So change canonical_key: `s = s.replace(\".\", \"\")` instead of replace(\".\", \" \").\n   Check side effects: \"C.r.a.c. - GO\" → base \"C.r.a.c.\" → \"crac\" ✓ matches \"CRAC\" ✓. \n\n2. `canonical_key(\"Nacional (URU)\")` expected \"nacional\" — got \"nacionaluru\" (parens→space→tokens). Again invalid test — code is parsed out first. Fix test to use parse_team_name base.\n\n3. `test_display_names_readable`: expected display \"Flamengo-RJ\" for key — got? register(\"Flamengo-RJ\",\"Flamengo\",\"Gremio\",\"Grêmio - RS\") → flamengo states {RJ}? No PI variant here → len(states)==1 → key \"flamengo\" (state dropped) → display \"Flamengo-RJ\"?? _pick_display: counts: \"Flamengo\" 2, display: no suffix (unambiguous) → \"Flamengo\". So display(reg.key_for(\"Flamengo-RJ\")) = \"Flamengo\" not \"Flamengo-RJ\". Test expectation wrong. Fix: assert display in (\"Flamengo\", \"Flamengo-RJ\")? Better assert it STARTS with \"Flamengo\" and accent handling: display for gremio in (\"Grêmio\",\"Gremio\") ✓.\n\n4. `test_every_match_has_scores_and_dates`: one Série A 2016 match with None goals — a real data gap (probably a W.O./abandoned match with empty score). Which row? From Brasileirao_Matches or BR-Football with empty goals. My loader: `parse_int(r[\"home_goal\"])` → None if \"-\"/empty. Find it and handle: keep the match but has_result()=False (already handles). The TEST should tolerate matches without results: assert *almost all* have scores (e.g. > 99.9%) rather than all. Let me check how many have missing goals.\n\nAlso there might be one \"NA\" datetime row in Libertadores (Flamengo-Athletico, goals \"-\") — that's a \"TBD\" row: season \"NA\", goals \"-\" — it's actually the 2023 final placeholder? No — it has stage \"final\" — probably a row for an unplayed fixture. Its date is None. My missing_dates <= 1 accounts for it. For goals: count missing-goal rows.\n\nLet me check data quickly, then fix tests + canonical_key.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b0ea5780012uJZwfK3WqapIb","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","messageID":"msg_05b0df8e5001HILMoAdyAoMmL8"}}
