,\"\") → \"vitoria f c\" → split → [vitoria, f, c] — \"f\" and \"c\" separately, not \"fc\"! The dots in \"F. C.\" separate the letters. Fix: collapse single-letter tokens? Join single letters into adjacent tokens? Common convention: \"F. C.\" = \"FC\". Approach: after dot-removal, merge consecutive single-character tokens: \"f\",\"c\" → \"fc\". Then strip. Implement: iterate tokens; merge runs of single-char tokens: [vitoria, f, c] → [vitoria, fc]; then strip club tokens → [vitoria] ✓. Also \"S. E. Palmeiras\" → [se, palmeiras] → merge singles: \"se\"+\"...\" hmm [s,e,palmeiras]? \"S. E.\" → s,e singles → merged \"se\" → then leading \"se\" not a club token — stays \"sepalmeiras\". Eh, fine.\n\nBut careful: merging single letters also affects \"4 de julho\" → [4, de, julho] — \"4\" single merges with \"de\"? Rule: merge CONSECUTIVE single-char tokens into one: [4, de, julho] → \"4\"+\"de\" = \"4de\" then julho → \"4dejulho\" — hmm that changes the alias key \"ivdejulho\"/\"4dejulho\"! My alias key \"4dejulho\" — with merging [4,de,julho] → 4de + julho = \"4dejulho\" — SAME ✓ lucky. But generally, merging adjacent singles could shift keys. Where does this occur? Club names with dots: \"A.b.c.\" [a,b,c] → \"abc\" ✓, \"C.r.b.\" → \"crb\" ✓, \"C.s.a.\" → \"csa\" ✓, \"C. R. B.\" → \"crb\" ✓. Any name where consecutive singles exist naturally without dots? \"4 de Julho\": 4(single), de(2), julho(4) — 4 is single, de is 2-char → run = [\"4\"] alone → \"4\" merged alone = \"4\" (no adjacent single) ✓ no change. Good — merging only affects runs of ≥2 single tokens (dot-separated initials).\n\nSo: merge consecutive runs of single-char tokens first, then strip club tokens.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b0fd69a001u1aOxgqWBAUODi","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","messageID":"msg_05b0f5c98001f1DMh0TlCqmndt"}}
