{"type":"text","timestamp":1788235019556,"sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","part":{"id":"prt_05b1cbf66001e674M8JoijwaRN","messageID":"msg_05b1be8d1001HEzVzAj4byczYE","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","type":"text","text":"anyio cancel-scopes require enter/exit in the same task. Restructuring the e2e tests to run each lifecycle in a single `asyncio.run` (simple and robust):","time":{"start":1788235005798,"end":1788235019552}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788235019556,"sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_31202d516e944a18bec206d0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"\"\"\"End-to-end test: the real MCP server over stdio, driven by an MCP client.\n\nEach test spawns the server once (stdio) and drives it with the official\nMCP client; the whole client lifecycle runs inside a single ``asyncio.run``\ntask because anyio cancel-scopes must be entered and exited in one task.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport asyncio\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom typing import Awaitable, Callable\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nSERVER = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))),\n                      \"server.py\")\nREPO = os.path.dirname(SERVER)\n\nEXPECTED_TOOLS = {\n    \"search_matches\", \"get_head_to_head\", \"get_team_stats\", \"get_team_history\",\n    \"list_teams\", \"get_competitions\", \"get_standings\", \"compare_seasons\",\n    \"search_players\", \"get_player\", \"search_players_at_club\",\n    \"get_statistics\", \"get_derbies\", \"answer_question\",\n}\n\n\nasync def _with_session(do: Callable[[ClientSession], Awaitable]):\n    params = StdioServerParameters(command=sys.executable, args=[SERVER], cwd=REPO)\n    async with stdio_client(params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            return await do(session)\n\n\ndef run_with_server(do):\n    return asyncio.run(_with_session(do))\n\n\ndef call_tool(tool: str, args: dict) -> dict:\n    async def _do(session: ClientSession):\n        res = await session.call_tool(tool, args)\n        return json.loads(res.content[0].text)\n    return run_with_server(_do)\n\n\ndef test_server_lists_all_tools():\n    async def _do(session: ClientSession):\n        res = await session.list_tools()\n        return {t.name for t in res.tools}\n    assert EXPECTED_TOOLS <= run_with_server(_do)\n\n\ndef test_search_matches_tool():\n    data = call_tool(\"search_matches\",\n                     {\"team\": \"Flamengo\", \"season\": 2023, \"limit\": 5})\n    assert data[\"total\"] > 0\n    assert data[\"matches\"][0][\"season\"] == 2023\n\n\ndef test_head_to_head_tool():\n    data = call_tool(\"get_head_to_head\",\n                     {\"team_a\": \"Flamengo\", \"team_b\": \"Fluminense\"})\n    assert data[\"derby\"] == \"Fla-Flu\"\n    assert data[\"total_matches\"] > 30\n\n\ndef test_standings_tool():\n    data = call_tool(\"get_standings\",\n                     {\"competition\": \"Brasileirão Serie A\", \"season\": 2019,\n                      \"top\": 3})\n    assert data[\"champion\"] == \"Flamengo-RJ\"\n\n\ndef test_player_tool():\n    data = call_tool(\"get_player\", {\"name\": \"Neymar Jr\"})\n    assert data[\"name\"] == \"Neymar Jr\"\n    assert data[\"overall\"] == 92\n\n\ndef test_answer_question_tool():\n    data = call_tool(\"answer_question\",\n                     {\"question\": \"Who won the 2019 Brasileirão?\"})\n    assert data[\"result\"][\"champion\"] == \"Flamengo-RJ\"\n\n\ndef test_error_is_structured_json():\n    data = call_tool(\"get_team_stats\", {\"team\": \"Narnia United\"})\n    assert \"error\" in data\n\n\ndef test_status_resource():\n    from mcp.types import AnyUrl\n\n    async def _do(session: ClientSession):\n        res = await session.read_resource(AnyUrl(\"soccer://status\"))\n        return json.loads(res.contents[0].text)\n    data = run_with_server(_do)\n    assert data[\"matches\"] > 15000\n    assert data[\"players\"] == 18207\n","filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-0bivsr25/retort-e0c09c17c08e/tests/test_mcp_server.py"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-0bivsr25/retort-e0c09c17c08e/tests/test_mcp_server.py","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"tests/test_mcp_server.py","time":{"start":1788235019547,"end":1788235019554}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The anyio cancel-scope error: reading a resource spawns tasks whose lifecycles cross task boundaries in my manual `__aenter__/__aexit__` management — specifically the read_resource call seems to schedule work in a separate task (resource reading uses a task group?). Actually the \"exit cancel scope in a different task\" is raised during `cm.__aexit__` in my teardown — because `stdio_client.__aenter__` was awaited in task T1 (created by run_until_complete(_start())), and `__aexit__` runs in a DIFFERENT task T2 (run_until_complete(_stop())). anyio cancel scopes require enter/exit in the same task. call_tool/read_resource worked because they're within the same running loop... but the FIXTURE TEARDOWN breaks.\n\nAlso the ERROR is attached to the LAST test (test_status_resource) because teardown runs after it.\n\nFix: keep all async work in ONE task. Restructure: run the entire module's server session inside a single async task with an internal queue? Simpler: give each test its own server session? Spawning the server takes ~1s per test × 8 tests = 8s — acceptable but wasteful.\n\nCleaner: use a module-scoped event loop + run everything through ONE persistent task that processes calls from a queue:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef mcp():\n    loop = asyncio.new_event_loop()\n    queue_in = queue.Queue()  # (tool, args, fut)\n    async def _worker():\n        params = StdioServerParameters(...)\n        async with stdio_client(params) as (r, w):\n            async with ClientSession(r, w) as s:\n                await s.initialize()\n                ready.set_result... # signal ready\n                while True:\n                    kind, payload = await loop-bound queue get...\n```\n\nDoable but fiddly. Alternative pragmatic approach: pytest-asyncio not installed... but `anyio` pytest plugin IS installed (anyio ships a pytest plugin, listed in pytest plugins: \"anyio-4.14.2\"!). I saw \"plugins: cov, anyio, bdd\" — the anyio pytest plugin provides @pytest.mark.anyio with fixture \"anyio_backend\". With that, I can write async tests with module-scoped... anyio fixture scoping is function-based though — a module-scoped session across async tests still has the enter/exit-per-test problem.\n\nSimplest robust: make each test independent BUT reuse one loop within the test — i.e., module fixture spins ONE background thread running the loop with the client session for the entire module, tests submit coroutines via asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe. Enter/exit all happen inside the SAME worker task/thread:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")\ndef mcp():\n    loop = asyncio.new_event_loop()\n    started = threading.Event()\n    import queue as _q\n    jobs = _q.Queue()\n\n    def _run_loop():\n        asyncio.set_event_loop(loop)\n        loop.run_until_complete(_main())\n\n    async def _main():\n        params = StdioServerParameters(command=sys.executable, args=[SERVER], cwd=REPO)\n        async with stdio_client(params) as (r, w):\n            async with ClientSession(r, w) as session:\n                await session.initialize()\n                started.set()\n                while True:\n                    item = await loop.run_in_executor(None, jobs.get)\n                    if item is None:\n                        break\n                    coro_fn, fut = item\n                    try:\n                        fut.set_result(await coro_fn(session))\n                    except Exception as e:\n                        fut.set_exception(e)\n\n    thread = threading.Thread(target=_run_loop, daemon=True)\n    thread.start()\n    started.wait(30)\n\n    holder = {\n        \"call\": lambda coro_fn: _submit(loop, jobs, coro_fn),\n    }\n    yield holder\n    jobs.put(None)\n    thread.join(30)\n    loop.close()\n\ndef _submit(loop, jobs, coro_fn):\n    fut = asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe? no — jobs queue consumed inside _main's loop.\n```\n\nThe submit from test thread: create concurrent.futures.Future, jobs.put((coro_fn, fut)); wait fut.result(timeout). Inside _main (running in the loop), `await coro_fn(session)` — same task context as the context managers ✓. `jobs.get` blocking the event loop — run_in_executor to avoid blocking; but concurrent gets... simple enough.\n\nHmm — but the fixture teardown: jobs.put(None) → worker breaks → exits context managers IN THE SAME task ✓ → thread ends ✓.\n\nThis works but adds threading complexity. Alternative much simpler: just give each test its own short-lived session via a helper context — 8 tests × ~1.2s spawn = ~10s. Meh.\n\nActually — even simpler: since only ONE test failed (resource read) and teardown after any test errors, note the REAL issue: teardown enters/exits in different tasks. I can avoid manual __aenter__ by running the whole session lifecycle inside ONE coroutine that also executes all test calls — i.e., collect calls via queue as above. OR: make the fixture function-scoped with `asyncio.run(...)` around each test: each test defines its async body and calls a helper `with_server(async_fn)` that does asyncio.run(full_lifecycle(async_fn)) — enter/exit and the call all in the same task inside asyncio.run ✓✓. Cost: server spawn per test (~1s × 8 = 8s). Reliability > speed. But loading the store per spawn is 0.6s + startup = each test ~2s → 16s total. Acceptable for an e2e test file.\n\nActually — middle ground: module-scoped via the threading pattern. It's ~30 lines. But complexity → fragility. The per-test pattern is dead simple and robust. Tests already took 1.06s... hmm actually module fixture total was ~1s?? The spawn+load seemed fast (0.6s load + transport). 8 tests × ~2s ≈ 16s — fine.\n\nLet me restructure: helper `run_with_server(async_fn)` — asyncio.run over a coroutine that opens the session, initializes, runs async_fn(session), tears down. Tests call run_with_server(lambda s: s.call_tool(...)).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05b1cf514001a4kUHlglEEsnAg","sessionID":"ses_fa52b88b3ffeUF09L5H3uZ1cg4","messageID":"msg_05b1be8d1001HEzVzAj4byczYE"}}
